      "calculationScope": "EXACT_MANUAL_CALCULATION",
      "manualArithmetic": "No assigned numerical exercise. For uniform perpendicular velocity, swept volume A*v*dt gives Q=A*v. Independent area integral of parabolic profile vmax(1-r²/R²) gives Q=pi*R²*vmax/2 and area mean vmax/2. Section-uniform density gives mass flux rho*Q. Stationary no-leak balance gives rho1*A1*v1=rho2*A2*v2; equal densities permit volume continuity even if viscous. Reviewer probes only:4cm³/s through4 and1cm² gives1 and4cm/s;400 parallel branches eachradiusR/10 have totalarea4piR²,so common totalQ needs one-quarter speed. A filling tank retains storage:3-1mL/s for5s adds10mL.",
      "manualFeasibility": "PASS_AI_EXACT",
      "physicalAssumptions": "Positive control-section area and positive elapsed time;normal component and area average,not profile peak. Finite-interval volume/time is time-averaged flow if unsteady. Scalar rho*Q and one-dimensional rho*A*v require uniform density on each section,as in the homogeneous-liquid introductory model;they are not generalized to correlated nonuniform density/velocity without the mass-flux integral. Stationary no-leak regions have no mass storage;volume continuity additionally needs common constant density. Viscosity does not invalidate mass conservation. Parallel branches use summed areas;filling includes accumulation.",
